**Key idea.** Reduce to the case $\gcd(n,10)=1$ by appending zeros (which preserves digit sum and supplies factors of $2$ and $5$). In the coprime case, use that $10$ has period $T=\operatorname{ord}_{n}(10)\ge 2$ modulo $n$, so digit positions split into classes mod $T$; positions in class $0$ contribute $1\bmod n$ and positions in class $1$ contribute $10\bmod n$. The congruence $M\equiv 0\pmod n$ then becomes $(k-s)+10s\equiv 0$, i.e. $9s\equiv -k\pmod n$, solvable because $\gcd(9,n)=1$ (from $3\nmid n$), with $0\le s\le n-1\le k-1$ (from $k\ge n$). Placing digit-sum $k-s$ in class $0$ and $s$ in class $1$ gives the result.

