## Proof

Write $n=2^{a}5^{b}\,n'$ with $\gcd(n',10)=1$. Since $3\nmid n$, also $3\nmid n'$. Put $c=\max(a,b)$.

**Reduction.** It suffices to find a positive integer $M$ with $n'\mid M$ and digit sum $k$; then $m:=M\cdot 10^{c}$ is divisible by $2^{a}5^{b}$ (because $10^{c}$ is) and by $n'$ (because $\gcd(10^{c},n')=1$), hence by $n$, and $\operatorname{digsum}(m)=\operatorname{digsum}(M)=k$. Since $k\ge n=2^{a}5^{b}n'\ge n'$, the target digit sum satisfies $k\ge n'$. Thus we are reduced to:

> **Claim.** Let $N\ge 1$ with $\gcd(N,10)=1$ and $3\nmid N$. For every $k\ge N$ there exists $M>0$ with $N\mid M$ and $\operatorname{digsum}(M)=k$.

**Proof of Claim.** If $N=1$, take $M=\underbrace{11\ldots 1}_{k\text{ ones}}$ (digit sum $k$, divisible by $1$).

Assume $N\ge 2$. Let $T=\operatorname{ord}_{N}(10)$. We have $T\ge 2$: otherwise $10\equiv 1\pmod N$, so $N\mid 9$, forcing $N\in\{1,3,9\}$, contradicting $N\ge 2$ and $3\nmid N$.

Since $3\nmid N$, we have $\gcd(9,N)=1$, so $9$ is invertible modulo $N$. Let $s$ be the unique integer in $\{0,1,\dots,N-1\}$ with
$$9s\equiv -k \pmod N.$$
Because $k\ge N$, we have $s\le N-1\le k-1$, hence $k-s\ge 1$.

Now construct $M$ by prescribing its decimal digits. Partition positions $\{0,1,2,\dots\}$ by their residue mod $T$:

- **Class $0$** (positions $0,T,2T,\dots$): here $10^{jT}\equiv (10^{T})^{j}\equiv 1\pmod N$.
- **Class $1$** (positions $1,T+1,2T+1,\dots$): here $10^{jT+1}\equiv 10\pmod N$.

Write $k-s=9q_{0}+r_{0}$ with $0\le r_{0}\le 8$, and $s=9q_{1}+r_{1}$ with $0\le r_{1}\le 8$. Place digit $9$ in positions $0,T,\dots,(q_{0}-1)T$ and, if $r_{0}>0$, digit $r_{0}$ in position $q_{0}T$ (class $0$). Place digit $9$ in positions $1,T+1,\dots,(q_{1}-1)T+1$ and, if $r_{1}>0$, digit $r_{1}$ in position $q_{1}T+1$ (class $1$). Set all other digits to $0$.

These positions are all distinct: classes $0$ and $1$ are disjoint since $T\ge 2$, and within each class the positions are distinct multiples of $T$. Every placed digit lies in $\{1,\dots,9\}$, so no carries occur. Since $k-s\ge 1$, the units position (position $0$) receives a positive digit, so $M\ge 1$.

The digit sum is
$$\operatorname{digsum}(M)=(k-s)+s=k.$$
Modulo $N$, all class-$0$ positions contribute $1$ and all class-$1$ positions contribute $10$, so
$$M\equiv (k-s)\cdot 1+s\cdot 10 = k+9s\equiv k+(-k)\equiv 0\pmod N.$$
Thus $N\mid M$, proving the Claim.

**Conclusion.** With $M$ from the Claim (applied to $N=n'$, using $k\ge n\ge n'$), set $m=M\cdot 10^{c}$. Then $n\mid m$ and $\operatorname{digsum}(m)=k$, with $m>0$. $\blacksquare$

